{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 4, :comments ["<b>And Balak son of Zippor was king of Moab at that time.</b> According to what we explained above — that Balak took note of what Israel did to Sihon the Amorite with sword and bow — the question arises: what distinguishes this day from any other, and why was he not roused to make plots against them long ago? Therefore he answered and said that 'at that time' they appointed him king for a temporary need, as Rashi explains. But according to the plain sense it is difficult: what does the phrase 'at that time' come to teach? And some say it comes to explain why it says 'and Moab was afraid' and not Balak — because they appointed him only 'at that time,' temporarily, so he said in his heart: what is this trouble to me? Nevertheless, outwardly, because of the people's fear, he had to do what he did." "And some say it comes to explain why Moab would be afraid, since it was already stated (Deuteronomy 2:9): ״אַל תָּצַר אֶת מוֹאָב״. Therefore it says: because Balak was king of Moab 'at that time,' and Balak was an Aramean and not a Moabite, as it is stated: ״אֶרֶץ בְּנֵי עַמּוֹ״ — Rashi explains that Balak was from there, and this is the land of Aram, as it is written: ״מִן אֲרָם יַנְחֵנִי בָלָק״. And they were afraid lest the whole land be called by Balak's name, just as Ammon and Moab were cleansed through Sihon, as it is written (Numbers 21:26): ״וְהוּא נִלְחַם בְּמֶלֶךְ מוֹאָב הָרִאשׁוֹן״ and so forth — so too the land would be cleansed for Israel by means of Balak who was their king 'at that time'; therefore ״עַתָּה יְלַחֲכוּ״ and not before. And some say: they will lick up all our surroundings and not touch us at all; rather they will make an end of all our surroundings and thereby we will have no survival. And it appears to me that this is why it says ״כִּלְחֹךְ הַשּׁוֹר אֵת יֶרֶק הַשָּׂדֶה״ — specifically the green of the field, which is the surroundings of the city, but they will not touch the green of the cities themselves."], :book :numbers, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 22, :notes {0 "The antecedent of 'he answered and said' and 'he had to do what he did' is inferred from context; the Hebrew uses impersonal/constructional forms without explicit subjects in several clauses."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:42:29.159415758Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}